definition.raw:
define a character $\phi_i'$ of $\mathsf{K}^{i+1}$ by 
\begin{equation}\label{E:phi'1}
\phi_i'(kj)=\phi_i(k)\hat\phi_i(j),
\end{equation}
for each $k\in \mathsf{K}^i$, $j\in \mathsf{J}^{i+1}$.

proposition.raw:
\label{P:line}
Let $g \in G$. Suppose that there exists $z\in [x,g^{-1}y]\cap \overline{\Omega(x,s_0)}$ such that $T \not\subseteq G_z$.  Then no irreducible subrepresentation of $\tau(g)$
is a type.

example.raw:
\label{Ex:1}
Consider $G=\mathrm{Sp}_4(F)$, given in matrix form relative to $J=\left[\begin{smallmatrix}0&I\\-I&0\end{smallmatrix}\right]\in M_4(F)$ 
as the set
$$
G = \{ g \in \mathrm{GL}_4(F)\mid {}^tgJg=J\}
$$
where ${}^tg$ denotes the transpose.  Note that $G$ contains a generalized Levi subgroup $G'$ isomorphic to $\mathrm{SL}_2(F)\times \mathrm{SL}_2(F)$, defined by the long roots.

Let $\varpi$ be a uniformizer of $F$.  For $i=1,2$, we choose an anisotropic torus $T_i$ of $\mathrm{SL}_2(F)$ that isomorphic to the norm-one elements of some quadratic ramified extension $E_i=F[\sqrt{\gamma_i\varpi}]$ with $\nu(\gamma_i)=0$.  Let $T=T_1\times T_2$ be the corresponding anisotropic torus of $G'$; explicitly we embed $T$ into $G$ as the subgroup
$$
T=\left\{ 
\bmat{a &0 &\phantom{b}b\phantom{b}&\phantom{b}0\phantom{b}\\ 
0&c&0&d\\ 
b\gamma_1\varpi &0&a&0\\ 0&d\gamma_2\varpi & 0& c}
\;\; \middle| \;\;\parbox{1.2in}{$a,b,c,d \in {\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}}, \quad\quad$ 
$a^2-b^2\gamma_1\varpi = 1$, $c^2-d^2\gamma_2\varpi=1$}\right\}.
$$
For each $i$, the point in the building $\mathcal{B}(\mathrm{SL}_2,F)$ corresponding to $T_i$ is the midpoint of the fundamental alcove.  Thus the point $x$ representing $\mathcal{B}(T)\subset \mathcal{B}(G)$ is the midpoint of the diagonal facet $\mathcal{F}$ in the closure of the fundamental alcove $\mathcal{C}$ in the standard apartment ${\mathcal{A}}$ of $\mathcal{B}(G)$, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{Fig:1}.  Evidently $\overline{\mathcal{F}} \subseteq {\mathcal{A}}^T$.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\begin{tikzpicture}


\draw[fill=gray!20,ultra thick, gray!20] (1,1) rectangle (4,4);


\draw (-4,1)--(9,1);
\draw (-4,4)--(9,4);
\draw (1,0)--(1,5);
\draw (4,0)--(4,5);
\draw (-2,0)--(-2,5);
\draw (7,0)--(7,5);
\draw (0,0)--(5,5);
\draw (-3,5)--(2,0);
\draw (3,5)--(8,0);
\draw (6,0)--(9,3);
\draw (-4,2)--(-1,5);


\draw[fill] (2.5,2.5) circle [radius=2pt];
\node[above left]  at (2.5,2.5) {$x$};

\draw[fill] (4,1) circle [radius=2pt];
\node[below right]  at (4,1) {$y$};

\draw[fill] (1,4) circle [radius=2pt];
\node[above left]  at (1,4) {$wy$};

\node at (3.3,1.8) {$\mathcal{C}$};
\node at (1.7,3.5) {$w\mathcal{C}$};

\node at (3.5,2.7) {$\mathcal{F}$};
\draw[thick,->] (3.55,2.95) arc (0:75:0.3cm);


\draw[dotted] (1.9,1.9) rectangle (3.1,3.1);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{A portion of the standard apartment ${\mathcal{A}}$ of $\mathrm{Sp}_4(F)$, identifying an alcove $\mathcal{C}$, the point $x$ that is the image of $\mathcal{B}(T)$ in $\mathcal{B}(G)$, the facet $\mathcal{F}$ containing $x$ and the vertex $y$ of $\mathcal{C}$ not in $\overline{\mathcal{F}}$.  The images of $y$ and of $\mathcal{C}$ under a reflection $w$ in the Weyl group are also indicated.  The subset ${\mathcal{A}}^T$ of ${\mathcal{A}}$ fixed by $T$ is the closed region shaded in gray.  For sake of example, the set $\Omega_{\apart}(x,s_0)$, with $s_0=\frac{1}{10}$, is indicated with a dotted line; its closure is ${\mathcal{A}}^T$.}
\label{Fig:1}
\end{figure}

Let us now prove that ${\mathcal{A}}^T \neq \overline{\mathcal{F}}$.  Let $y$ denote the (non-special) vertex of $\overline{\mathcal{C}}$ opposite $\mathcal{F}$, and $w\in G$ a reflection in the wall of ${\mathcal{A}}$ containing $\mathcal{F}$ (viewed as a representative of the corresponding element of the affine Weyl group).  Adopting the convention that a matrix ring stands for its intersection with $\mathrm{Sp}_4(F)$, we compute directly that
\begin{equation}\label{E:GCGy}
G_{\mathcal{C}} = \bmat{{\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}} & {\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}} & {\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}} & {\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}} \\ \mathfrak{p}  & {\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}} & {\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}}&{\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}} \\ \mathfrak{p} &\mathfrak{p} & {\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}}&\mathfrak{p}\\ \mathfrak{p}&\mathfrak{p}&{\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}}&{\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}}}
\quad \text{and} \quad
G_{w\mathcal{C}} = 
\bmat{{\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}} & \mathfrak{p} & {\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}} & {\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}} \\ 
{\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}}  & {\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}} & {\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}}&{\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}} \\ 
\mathfrak{p} &\mathfrak{p} & {\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}}&{\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}}\\ 
\mathfrak{p}&\mathfrak{p}&\mathfrak{p}&{\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}}},
\end{equation}
each of which contains $T$ as a subgroup.  It therefore follows that $\{y,wy\} \subset \overline{\mathcal{C}}\cup \overline{w\mathcal{C}}\subseteq {\mathcal{A}}^T$, even though neither $y$ nor $wy$ lie in $\overline{\mathcal{F}}$.
Applying now Lemma~\ref{L:strongunicityfails}, we deduce that strong unicity fails for any supercuspidal representation $\pi(\Psi)$ constructed from $G^0=T$.

